{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time series shapelet classification time series shapelet classification","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","fileName":"Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Series Shapelet Classification","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-14 21:00\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Sequence and Network Classification]] · [[Time-Series Feature Engineering]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequence and Network Classification","Time-Series Feature Engineering","dataclassification.pdf","featureengineeringformachinelearninganddataanalytics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time series shapelet classification time series shapelet classification","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","fileName":"Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Series Shapelet Classification","heading":"Time Series Shapelet Classification","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Time series shapelet classification uses short subsequences whose presence or close match is highly discriminative of a class. A candidate shapelet is compared with every possible window in a series, and the minimum distance becomes a feature or split criterion.\n\nShapelets provide localized explanations because the prediction can identify the characteristic segment. Searching the enormous space of possible subsequences requires pruning, sampling, or other acceleration.\n\nThe minimum distance from a candidate shapelet to all same-length windows in a series can become a derived feature. A shapelet transform therefore converts variable temporal structure into a conventional tabular representation while preserving a discriminative local form.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequence and Network Classification","Time-Series Feature Engineering","dataclassification.pdf","featureengineeringformachinelearninganddataanalytics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"time series shapelet classification time series shapelet classification","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","fileName":"Time Series Shapelet Classification.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Time Series Shapelet Classification","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[dataclassification.pdf]]\n\n[[featureengineeringformachinelearninganddataanalytics.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Sequence and Network Classification","Time-Series Feature Engineering","dataclassification.pdf","featureengineeringformachinelearninganddataanalytics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}